@@ -5,6 +5,13 @@
 
 ## 2026-09-15
 
+### 1930 — mac-keyvault T2: sign/verify go through the same trust gate as rotate, and the low-S rule is applied on the way out, not assumed from the bridge
+- FINDING: `SecKeyCreateSignature(kSecKeyAlgorithmECDSASignatureDigestX962SHA256)` signs the given 32 bytes as the digest (no re-hash: the DER verifies with `crypto/ecdsa` and `openssl dgst -sha256 -verify` over the original payload) and returns X9.62 DER that Go's `encoding/asn1` parses strictly; whether corecrypto folds s into the low half is not documented, so the vault does not assume it: every signature is parsed, folded to low-S (`normalized: true` when it was high), and re-verified under the record's own SPKI before it is emitted. The same non-assumption holds for `crypto/ecdsa`, which demonstrably emits high-S about half the time; the test fakes therefore pick the half deterministically instead of trusting the library.
+- DECISION: The operations gate is derived from the registry (`Operations()`), not re-implemented: `Manager.authorize` requires the operation to be in what `describe` prints and names the reason of a miss by its own code (`usage_refused`, `expired`, `unsupported_primitive` exit 1). Before that it runs the rev6/rev7 trust gate (`RecordProblem`, schema, `ValidateStored`), so a rev1 tag or a forged persisted field never reaches the private key (`metadata_unknown`) — the forgery table in `invariants_test.go` now drives `Sign` and `PublicKeyFor` as well as rotate/meta.
+- DECISION: `verify` has three outcomes with three exit codes: 0 verified; 1 `signature_invalid` (well-formed, does not match — the verdict is in `result.verified`, the error contract still carries code/hint); 3 `invalid_signature` (not strict DER / not 64 raw bytes / r,s outside [1,n-1]) or `high_s_refused` (valid but malleable; `--allow-high-s` opts in, needed for openssl-made signatures). A `@FILE` argument is always raw bytes, never hex-decoded, so a 32-byte digest that happens to look like ASCII hex is not misread (mutant M10).
+- FINDING: Two mutants survive and state bounds, not gaps: M3 (strict re-encode check removed) survives because `encoding/asn1` already refuses non-minimal integers and long-form lengths one layer down — the re-encode check is defence in depth; M12 (CLI digest-length check removed) survives because `Manager.Sign` validates the digest before listing — the combined M12c (both gates after the listing) is killed by the CLI store-untouched assertion.
+- SCOPE: `TASK-260915-nl5may`: `internal/keyvault/ecdsa.go` (new), `keyvault.go` (`Backend.Sign`, `authorize`, `Sign`, `PublicKeyFor`), `registry.go` (via sign/verify), `security_darwin.{c,h,go}` + `security_unsupported.go` (bridge), `cmd/mac-keyvault/main.go` (`sign`, `verify`), tests, README, SKILL. kvctl's verifier is not in this repository; interop is proven against Go `crypto/ecdsa` and openssl 3.6 instead (stated bound).
+
 ### 1845 — mac-keyvault rev7: a validator can only judge what the decoder kept — the persisted record decoder is strict about unknown members
 - FINDING: Review rev6 (F14) reproduced the F4/F8/F12/F13 class one layer earlier: `DecodeSingleJSON` decoded a schema-2 tag without `DisallowUnknownFields`, so a persisted member the schema does not define (`future_top_level_policy`, `format.future_format_policy`) was erased by `encoding/json` before `ValidateStored` ran; `Manager.Rotate` reproduced the record minus the member (`creates=1`) and `MetaSet` rewrote the tag without it. The one-row-per-field invariant table reflects over the decoded Go struct and is blind to JSON members that never reach the struct — the reviewer's control named exactly that blind spot.
 - DECISION: The strictness lives in the decoder, not in another table row. `DecodeSingleJSON` now sets `DisallowUnknownFields` next to `UseNumber` and the EOF gate, so the one JSON boundary rule (rev5) covers members as well as documents; unknown top-level and nested struct members make the tag an unreadable record (schema 0, `record_unreadable` finding, `rotate`/`meta set|unset` refuse with `metadata_unknown`, zero creates/updates, old tag byte-identical). `Record.Meta`, `--meta-json` (`map[string]any`) and `--json-value` (`any`) are open targets and unaffected — the positive controls store the same member names inside `meta` and still rotate. A field that starts driving behaviour is added to the struct with a schema bump, as model v2 §2–§3 say; until then it cannot be silently downgraded.
